**Heuristic quality rules** are simple statistics with thresholds. The Gopher paper (DeepMind, 2021) applied these to MassiveWeb, the web part of its MassiveText corpus. A document is removed if:

- it has fewer than 50 or more than 100,000 words, or a mean word length outside 3 to 10 characters;
- its ratio of hash symbols or of ellipses to words is above 0.1;
- over 90 percent of its lines start with a bullet, or over 30 percent end with an ellipsis;
- under 80 percent of its words contain a letter;
- it contains fewer than 2 of the **stop words** *the, be, to, of, and, that, have, with* (stop words are the most common function words of a language; a real English text cannot avoid them).

A separate repetition filter removes documents with too many repeated lines or n-grams.
